<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saruj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Suruç, Serugh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saruj, now Suruç in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, is an ancient town on the plain south-west of Edessa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Şanlıurfa</a>), near the Syrian border. Known in antiquity as Batnae, it was a stage on routes across upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. In the 11th century it was held by the Numayrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and from 1101 it belonged to the crusader County of Edessa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saruj.jpg" alt="Saruj" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VOA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suru%C3%A7_town_hall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suruç lies on a plain near the Syrian border, 46 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Urfa</a>, in a fertile district well suited to fruit and grapevines. It sits between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> on the west and Urfa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> on the east, about a day's journey from both cities by pre-industrial transport, and this traffic brought it a measure of commercial prosperity. It was also a post station between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> and Sumaysat. The town itself was mainly agricultural: in the 12th century Ibn Jubayr described orchards and irrigation channels within it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was held successively by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. In the Roman and Byzantine periods the town was called Batnae. Constantine the Great, who reigned from 306 to 337, placed it under the control of Edessa, and in 363 Emperor Julian visited it on his march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Batnae became a suffragan bishopric of Edessa. Its best-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> hymnographer Jacob of Serugh, was born around 451, became bishop at the age of 67 and died around 521; he wrote hymns, 760 homilies and a Syriac translation of Evagrius, and both Chalcedonian and non-Chalcedonian Christians claim him.</p><p>The Arabic name Saruj became prominent after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquests</a> of the 7th century, and the town surrendered in 639 to Iyad ibn Ghanm. In the 900s it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a> and was later taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. In the late 1090s a civil war between the Seljuk princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> let the Artuqid prince Sökmen set up a short-lived principality there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> captured it in 1101, and for almost half a century it belonged to the crusader County of Edessa. The poet al-Hariri made the hero of his maqamat, Abu Zayd al-Saruji, a native of Saruj driven out by the Christians. Imad ad-Din Zangi took the town in January 1145.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abu'l-Fida described the town as lying in ruins in the 1300s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> brought the area into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1517. In late Ottoman times Suruç was the seat of a kaymakam. The visitor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> wrote that the plain was first inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Turkomans, but found it in the 17th century mainly settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Turkomans. Suruç is now a municipality and district of Şanlıurfa Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, inhabited mostly by Kurds and Turks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suruç” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Bible, the Peshitta, was translated from the 2nd century onwards; the poet and theologian Ephrem the Syrian, who died in 373, wrote hymns that are still sung; and writers such as Aphrahat, Jacob of <b>Serugh</b> and, in the 13th century, Bar Hebraeus produced theology, history and science.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 990, as Hamdanid power faded, their chief Waththab ibn Sabiq seized Harran, and the tribe went on to hold <b>Saruj</b>, Raqqa and for a time Edessa.</blockquote><a href="/state/numayrids">Numayrids</a></li></ul></section>
